This chapter introduces the concepts to which every later chapter refers. It covers molecular representations, which turn a molecule into a fixed-length vector of features, the model families that learn from them, and the assumption that similar molecules have similar properties, together with the cases in which this assumption breaks down. It then turns to label noise, describing its sources in real assay data, the seven artificial noise conditions used in this thesis and the methods for LNL. The chapter closes with NoiseInject, the package that injects the noise and reports robustness and uncertainty metrics, and with the metrics used throughout.




\section{Molecular Representations in QSAR}
