No single method is expected to win, since averaged over all problems no learning algorithm outperforms another \citep{Wolpert1996, Wolpert1997}. The analog in chemistry is a study of twelve algorithms on twelve bioactivity datasets with simulated experimental errors at ten noise levels, which found that no single algorithm was best overall at maintaining predictive performance, and that some algorithms do better or worse at different levels of noise \citep{Cortes2015}. No single method can therefore be assumed to suit every dataset. Neighbor-based cleaning dates back to editing a training set by its nearest neighbors \citep{Wilson1972}, and it relies on similar molecules having similar labels, an assumption that activity cliffs break. QSAR models also frequently fail to predict activity cliffs \citep{Dablander2023}. Chapter~\ref{chap:flaws} tests the assumption that similar molecules have similar labels, and Chapter~\ref{chap:similarity} tests neighbor-based noise mitigation with the hybrid as the feature space.


