Of the three conditions run on every model, grouped-shifted noise is the hardest on every dataset: it gave the lowest \aucnorm{} for nearly every model and representation, the main exceptions being the two VBLL networks on LogD and Caco-2. Under this condition RF is the most robust model on hERG $K_i$ and LogD and has the highest predictive performance on hERG $K_i$.

LightGBM has particularly varied results across the datasets. On LogD, it has relatively high predictive performance and middle-of-the-pack noise robustness. However, on hERG $K_i$ and Caco-2, it loses the most with increased noise, particularly under Gaussian and grouped-shifted noise. The effects are not as pronounced under grouped-wider noise, although it still ranks among the least robust there. XGBoost degrades in the same way on hERG $K_i$ and Caco-2.

