While the performance is not identical between the datasets, some rankings hold across them (Figure~\ref{fig:transfer}, which ranks all 19 models; the ranks below are among the thirteen base models). RF is the most consistent: among the thirteen base models under Gaussian noise it ranks between first and seventh for \aucnorm{} on every dataset and representation, second or third on QM9 and first on LogD at five of six. The GP has the highest predictive performance on clean labels on all three assay datasets with nearly every representation, and ranks third to ninth for \aucnorm. NGBoost, the most robust model on QM9, ranks first to third on LogD, second to tenth on Caco-2 and sixth to ninth on hERG $K_i$. LightGBM falls furthest, ranking 10th to 13th on Caco-2 and hERG $K_i$ at every representation while being among the highest in predictive performance on clean labels. VBLL-$\alpha$ is the opposite, ranking first or second for \aucnorm{} on hERG $K_i$ and last on LogD at every representation, from one of the lowest clean $R^2$ values. Its robustness depends on the dataset rather than on the representation.

