Across the four datasets, the base models fall into two groups (Table~\ref{tab:leaders}). The forests, RF and QRF, are reliably robust. They rank among the most robust base models on every dataset under each noise condition run on all models, with Caco-2 under grouped-shifted noise the weakest case. They also lead predictive performance under grouped-shifted noise on QM9 and hERG $K_i$, although their predictive performance on clean labels is middling. The other models perform well only under some conditions, and several of them are where the interaction between model and representation in the variance decomposition comes from. The GP has the highest or close to the highest predictive performance on clean labels and under Gaussian noise on every assay dataset, and changes little with representation, but under grouped-shifted noise it matches the leading model only on LogD. Its \aucnorm{} is middling because it starts from the highest $R^2$ and has the most to lose. NGBoost is among the most robust models on QM9 and LogD, but it predicts well only with the right representation, such as PDV. SVM is middling for robustness but leads for predictive performance under grouped-shifted noise on LogD and under grouped-wider noise on hERG $K_i$. The plain and Bayesian networks have high predictive performance on clean labels, are the least robust, and depend most on representation. The VBLL networks are among the most robust on Caco-2 and hERG $K_i$, from low predictive performance on clean labels, and the least robust on LogD. LightGBM and XGBoost combine high predictive performance on clean labels with some of the lowest robustness on Caco-2 and hERG $K_i$. How much noise is expected matters as well. At a noise level of 0.5 the models still rank by predictive performance much as they do on clean labels, while by 1.0 the order has changed, most of all on Caco-2 and hERG $K_i$. The order of models by \aucnorm{} is broadly similar across noise conditions, with the forests near the top under each. On every dataset the leading pairings in the table are closer together than their range over replicates, so each row names a group of near-equivalent choices.

