We have identified flaws in the representations themselves and in the similarities computed over them. For a given molecule, its set of neighbors, built from one representation and one distance, is the input to several other processes such as predicting a molecule's activity from its nearest neighbors, detecting noisy labels by comparing a label against its neighbors' labels, defining an applicability domain by distance to the training set \citep{Tropsha2010}, grouping molecules into clusters for data splits \citep{Butina1999}, identifying activity cliffs, and, in federated learning, weighting each site's vote on a molecule by that molecule's distance to the site's own data \citep{Hanser2025}. If the distance is flawed, then every application of distance will suffer the consequences.

Section~\ref{sec:lnl_motivation} reports the exploratory LNL study, in which the 20 configurations with the lowest retention under noise all used a distance. Section~\ref{sec:flaws:neighbors} turns to noise detection by neighbors, the most direct use of similarity in this thesis. 

