The noise types differ in what they allow a method to correct. Under censoring, the location of the error (the limit) and its direction are known and only its size is unknown, which is why raising every clipped label by one shared amount recovered much of the lost performance. Outlier noise affects few labels, so the widened labels should stand out against their neighbors, although detection results are pending; removing or down-weighting them would still not recover the true values. Under grouped-shifted noise, molecules of the same scaffold family share one offset, so comparing a molecule with its neighbors cannot reveal it. Gaussian noise, the standard assumption in studies of noise robustness, is the hardest to correct, because every label carries an error of unknown size and sign, the neighbors used to judge a label are noisy too, and a large disagreement with them may be an activity cliff rather than an error. In the exploratory study of Chapter~\ref{chap:flaws}, only 8 of 860 cleaning configurations had positive recovery at both noise levels. A better similarity can make neighbors closer in true activity, but it cannot remove the noise on their labels.


