Noise mitigation changes, drops or reweights the labels once before training, so a wrong estimate stays for every later model; it is cheaper, since it is done once and reused by any model. Noise-robust training instead leaves the labels unchanged, adapts throughout training and uses all the data \citep{Song2022} (Section~\ref{sec:lnl_background}). Correcting labels directly is justified when the noise has a known direction, as in censoring, or affects a clearly separate subset, such as gross outliers or an offset tied to one source. In the exploratory study of Chapter~\ref{chap:flaws} (Section~\ref{sec:lnl_motivation}), the methods that held up estimated noise from the model's own loss or uncertainty, and they included one noise mitigation method, small-loss selection. Outside censoring and clearly separate subsets, we therefore recommend methods that judge labels by the model's own loss or uncertainty rather than by neighbors \textbf{[RESULT NEEDED: confirm against the Chapter 6 full runs]}. Either approach can be paired with uncertainty estimation where it is reliable. Mean predicted uncertainty rose with noise, and the aleatoric component can compare noise between datasets or noise levels, although it does not estimate a dataset's own error (Chapter~\ref{chap:robustness}). Outside censoring, however, per-molecule uncertainty did not identify the noisy labels, and under censoring the models were most confident on the clipped labels. Uncertainty should therefore be used to judge how noisy a dataset is, not which labels are wrong.

